# The harness tester: one NPC, one tree, every check that can be driven from a dialogue.
#
# Shipped in the harness's own asset pack, so LowTalk loads it straight from the jar. Nothing copies it to disk,
# which means it cannot fall out of step with the build the way the test corridor did.
#
# Two rules hold everywhere in this file, and the harness depends on both:
#
#   1. Every check gets a passage of its own, named check_<id with dots as underscores>. The harness watches
#      passages, so a shared passage would mark several checks run when only one was. No exceptions, even where
#      it makes the file longer.
#   2. A passage that shows something timed waits it out before returning. Two effects on screen at once make a
#      screenshot ambiguous about which one it caught.
#   3. An option that leads to an area may be guarded on what is left in that area. An option that leads to
#      another PAGE never is. The page links were once guarded on a hand-written sum of the prefixes behind
#      them, and adding four checks without extending the sum hid two whole pages of the tree from the person
#      walking it -- silently, because a hidden option looks exactly like a finished one.
#
# Checks needing a body in the world (blocks, triggers, restarts, the editor) are not here. They are in the
# check list as gaps with their own steps.

npc: @harness
title: LowTalk harness
speaker: Tester
include: _harness_shared

# A choice shows at most eight options, so the hub is two pages rather than one long list.

== start
Harness tester. Pick an area; each option runs one check and comes back.
-> What the player sees: notifications <<if remaining("notify") > 0>>
    <<jump area_notify>>
-> What the player sees: titles <<if remaining("title") > 0>>
    <<jump area_title>>
-> Items <<if remaining("items") > 0>>
    <<jump area_items>>
-> The player's body <<if remaining("body") > 0>>
    <<jump area_body>>
-> The world <<if remaining("world") > 0>>
    <<jump area_world>>
-> Media <<if remaining("media") > 0>>
    <<jump area_media>>
-> More...
    <<jump start_more>>
-> Leave
    <<end>>

== start_more
The rest: this NPC, the language itself, and what gets remembered.
-> This NPC <<if remaining("npc") > 0>>
    <<jump area_npc>>
-> Choices and flow <<if remaining("basics") > 0>>
    <<jump area_basics>>
-> Typed input <<if remaining("input") > 0>>
    <<jump area_input>>
-> Random and time <<if remaining("random") > 0>>
    <<jump area_random>>
-> Format extras <<if remaining("format") > 0>>
    <<jump area_format>>
-> Objectives and standing <<if remaining("progress") > 0>>
    <<jump area_progress>>
-> More...
    <<jump start_more2>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

# A choice shows at most eight options, so Memory moves here to make room for the player's own area.

== start_more2
What the player is: what they are called, what they may do, what they know, and what is remembered of them.
-> Memory <<if remaining("memory") > 0>>
    <<jump area_memory>>
-> The player themselves <<if remaining("player") + remaining("learn") > 0>>
    <<jump area_player>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

# ---- notifications ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_notify
Notifications appear in the corner and fade by themselves.
-> A plain toast <<if not checked("notify.toast")>>
    <<jump check_notify_toast>>
-> A warning toast <<if not checked("notify.warning")>>
    <<jump check_notify_warning>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_notify_toast
<<notify "LowTalk test" "Notification works" success>>
<<wait 3>>
<<jump area_notify>>

== check_notify_warning
<<notify "Careful" "This is the warning style" warning>>
<<wait 3>>
<<jump area_notify>>

# ---- titles -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_title
The styles are the game's own, so a server has the ones its version shipped: Default and Major everywhere, GoblinBreach and VoidEviction from 0.7. Each should look plainly different.
-> Default, no style named <<if not checked("title.default")>>
    <<jump check_title_default>>
-> Default written the old way, as "minor" <<if not checked("title.minor.alias")>>
    <<jump check_title_minor_alias>>
-> Major <<if not checked("title.major")>>
    <<jump check_title_major>>
-> GoblinBreach (0.7 and later) <<if has_title_style("GoblinBreach") and not checked("title.goblinbreach")>>
    <<jump check_title_goblinbreach>>
-> VoidEviction (0.7 and later) <<if has_title_style("VoidEviction") and not checked("title.voideviction")>>
    <<jump check_title_voideviction>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_title_default
<<title "Default title" "Secondary line">>
<<wait 3>>
<<jump area_title>>

== check_title_minor_alias
<<title "Written as minor" "Same as the default" minor>>
<<wait 3>>
<<jump area_title>>

== check_title_major
<<title "MAJOR TITLE" "The grove remembers" Major 4>>
<<wait 4>>
<<jump area_title>>

== check_title_goblinbreach
<<title "GOBLIN BREACH" "they come through the pass" GoblinBreach 4>>
<<wait 4>>
<<jump area_title>>

== check_title_voideviction
<<title "VOID EVICTION" "the dark lets go" VoidEviction 4>>
<<wait 4>>
<<jump area_title>>

# ---- items --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_items
You are holding {count("Food_Bread")} bread.
-> Give me two bread <<if not checked("items.give")>>
    <<jump check_items_give>>
-> Take one back <<if not checked("items.take")>>
    <<jump check_items_take>>
-> Read what I am holding <<if not checked("items.count")>>
    <<jump check_items_count>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_items_give
<<give Food_Bread 2>>
<<jump area_items>>

== check_items_take
<<take Food_Bread 1>>
<<jump area_items>>

== check_items_count
count() says {count("Food_Bread")}, and has("Food_Bread") is {has("Food_Bread")}.
<<jump area_items>>

# ---- the player's body --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_body
Health and effects.
-> Heal me fully <<if not checked("body.heal")>>
    <<jump check_body_heal>>
-> Set my health to 10 <<if not checked("body.stat")>>
    <<jump check_body_stat>>
-> Give me regeneration <<if not checked("body.effect")>>
    <<jump check_body_effect>>
-> Take it away again <<if not checked("body.cure")>>
    <<jump check_body_cure>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_body_heal
<<heal>>
<<jump area_body>>

== check_body_stat
<<stat Health 10>>
<<jump area_body>>

== check_body_effect
<<effect HealthRegen_Buff_T1>>
<<jump area_body>>

== check_body_cure
<<cure HealthRegen_Buff_T1>>
<<jump area_body>>

# ---- the world ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_world
It is hour {hour()} and the weather is {weather()}.
-> Make it rain <<if not checked("world.weather")>>
    <<jump check_world_weather>>
-> Clear the sky <<if not checked("world.weather.clear")>>
    <<jump check_world_weather_clear>>
-> Set it to noon <<if not checked("world.time")>>
    <<jump check_world_time>>
-> Read a translated string <<if not checked("world.translate")>>
    <<jump check_world_translate>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_world_weather
<<weather Zone1_Cloudy_Medium player>>
<<wait 2>>
<<jump area_world>>

== check_world_weather_clear
<<weather clear player>>
<<wait 2>>
<<jump area_world>>

== check_world_time
<<time noon>>
<<jump area_world>>

== check_world_translate
t() gives "{t("server.lowtalk.hint.talk")}", which should read as words rather than as the key itself.
<<jump area_world>>

# ---- media --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_media
Music, particles and the camera.
-> Play a music container <<if not checked("media.music")>>
    <<jump check_media_music>>
-> Back to the area's own music <<if not checked("media.music.clear")>>
    <<jump check_media_music_clear>>
-> Particles on this NPC <<if not checked("media.vfx")>>
    <<jump check_media_vfx>>
-> Shake my camera <<if not checked("media.camera")>>
    <<jump check_media_camera>>
-> Back
    <<jump start>>

== check_media_music
<<music MC_Zone0>>
<<wait 4>>
<<jump area_media>>

== check_media_music_clear
<<music clear>>
<<wait 2>>
<<jump area_media>>

== check_media_vfx
<<vfx Totem_Heal_Simple_Test 2 3>>
<<wait 3>>
<<jump area_media>>

== check_media_camera
<<camera Battleaxe_Bash>>
<<wait 2>>
<<jump area_media>>

# ---- this NPC -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_npc
This NPC's own name, state and animation. Renaming persists, so put it back when you are done.
-> Play an animation <<if not checked("npc.anim")>>
    <<jump check_npc_anim>>
-> Play a sound here <<if not checked("npc.sound")>>
    <<jump check_npc_sound>>
-> Rename me <<if not checked("npc.rename")>>
    <<jump check_npc_rename>>
-> Give me my name back <<if not checked("npc.rename.restore")>>
    <<jump check_npc_rename_restore>>
-> Turn hostile, then friendly again <<if not checked("npc.attitude")>>
    <<jump check_npc_attitude>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_npc_anim
<<anim Wave>>
<<wait 2>>
<<jump area_npc>>

== check_npc_sound
<<sound SFX_Player_Pickup_Item>>
<<jump area_npc>>

== check_npc_rename
<<npc_name "Renamed Tester">>
<<jump area_npc>>

== check_npc_rename_restore
<<npc_name "LowTalk harness">>
<<jump area_npc>>

== check_npc_attitude
My attitude to you is {attitude()}.
<<attitude hostile>>
Now hostile. Press Continue and I will calm down again.
<<attitude friendly>>
<<jump area_npc>>

# ---- choices and flow ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_basics
Options can be hidden, shown greyed out, or offered once. The reveal below sets a variable the next two read.
-> Two lines with a Continue between them <<if not checked("basics.continue")>>
    <<jump check_basics_continue>>
-> Reveal the hidden option <<if not checked("basics.reveal")>>
    <<jump check_basics_reveal>>
-> The hidden option (only here once revealed) <<if $harness.revealed>>
    <<jump check_basics_hidden>>
-> Greyed out until revealed <<show if $harness.revealed>>
    <<jump check_basics_disabled>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_basics_continue
# The one passage that speaks twice on purpose: the Continue button between two lines is what it checks.
The first of two lines. Expect a Continue button rather than the option list.
And the second. Expect the options back after this one.
<<jump area_basics>>

== check_basics_reveal
<<set $harness.revealed = true>>
Revealed. The hidden option is now in the list and the greyed one is clickable.
<<jump area_basics>>

== check_basics_hidden
You reached an option that was not in the list until a variable said so.
<<jump area_basics>>

== check_basics_disabled
This one was visible but greyed out before the reveal, and refused the click.
<<jump area_basics>>

# ---- typed input --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_input
A text box takes what you type; Enter and OK should behave the same.
-> Type a word <<if not checked("input.word")>>
    <<jump check_input_word>>
-> Type a number, and read plural() <<if not checked("input.plural")>>
    <<jump check_input_plural>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_input_word
<<input $tmp.word "Type any word, then press Enter">>
<<if $tmp.word == "">>
  The box came back empty. Expect this line when you submit nothing.
<<else>>
  You typed "{$tmp.word}". Expect it echoed exactly, capitals, spaces and all.
<<endif>>
<<jump area_input>>

== check_input_plural
<<input $tmp.n "Type a number">>
{$tmp.n} {plural($tmp.n, "apple", "apples")}. Expect the word to agree with the number, and 1 to read "apple".
<<jump area_input>>

# ---- random and time ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_random
Chance, dice and the clock.
-> Flip a coin with chance() <<if not checked("random.chance")>>
    <<jump check_random_chance>>
-> Roll a number with random() <<if not checked("random.number")>>
    <<jump check_random_number>>
-> Count visits with ordinal() <<if not checked("random.ordinal")>>
    <<jump check_random_ordinal>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_random_chance
<<if chance(0.5)>>
  HEADS. Expect roughly half of tries, and not the same answer every time.
<<else>>
  TAILS. Expect roughly half of tries, and not the same answer every time.
<<endif>>
<<jump area_random>>

== check_random_number
random(6) gave {random(6)}. Expect a whole number from 0 to 5, changing most tries.
<<jump area_random>>

== check_random_ordinal
<<set $npc.rolls = $npc.rolls + 1>>
This is the {ordinal($npc.rolls)} roll for this NPC, counted per NPC rather than per player.
<<jump area_random>>

# ---- format extras ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_format
[Greetings|Hello|Well met], {player}. Expect that word to vary between visits.
-> A random block picks one of three <<if not checked("format.variation")>>
    <<jump check_format_random>>
-> A once-only option <<once>>
    <<jump check_format_once>>
-> A two second pause <<if not checked("format.wait")>>
    <<jump check_format_wait>>
-> A line from an included file <<if not checked("format.include")>>
    <<jump check_format_include>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_format_random
<<random>>
  Alternative ONE. Expect a different one most times.
<<or>>
  Alternative TWO. Expect a different one most times.
<<or>>
  Alternative THREE. Expect a different one most times.
<<endrandom>>
<<jump area_format>>

== check_format_once
That option removes itself for good once picked. Look at the list: it has gone.
<<jump area_format>>

== check_format_wait
Counting... one...
<<wait 2>>
...two. Expect about two seconds between those lines, with no button in between.
<<jump area_format>>

# ---- objectives and standing --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_progress
Standing with my group is {reputation()}, rank "{rank()}". Objective_Gather reads "{objective("Objective_Gather")}".
-> Raise my standing by ten <<if not checked("progress.reputation.up")>>
    <<jump check_progress_reputation_up>>
-> Lower it by ten <<if not checked("progress.reputation.down")>>
    <<jump check_progress_reputation_down>>
-> Start Objective_Gather <<if not checked("progress.objective")>>
    <<jump check_progress_objective>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_progress_reputation_up
<<reputation +10>>
Expect the number above to rise by ten, and the rank to change at the boundary.
<<jump area_progress>>

== check_progress_reputation_down
<<reputation -10>>
Expect it to fall by ten.
<<jump area_progress>>

== check_progress_objective
<<objective Objective_Gather>>
Expect a tracker entry, and the state above to read "active". Objectives are rough on this version: if the client drops here, that is the game's tracker bug rather than this check.
<<jump area_progress>>

# ---- memory -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

== area_memory
Per NPC: we have met {$met ? "before" : "not before"}. Per player: you have opened a tester {$player.visits} time(s). Spawn a second tester with /harness npc and compare: the first line differs between them, the second does not.
-> Remember me <<if not checked("memory.npc")>>
    <<jump check_memory_npc>>
-> Count this visit, across every tester <<if not checked("memory.player")>>
    <<jump check_memory_player>>
-> Have I seen the marker passage? visited() says {visited("check_memory_marker")} <<if not checked("memory.visited")>>
    <<jump check_memory_visited>>
-> Go to the marker passage <<if not checked("memory.marker")>>
    <<jump check_memory_marker>>
-> Forget me again <<if not checked("memory.reset")>>
    <<jump check_memory_reset>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more>>

== check_memory_npc
<<set $met = true>>
Set on this NPC only. A second tester should still say we have not met.
<<jump area_memory>>

== check_memory_player
<<set $player.visits = $player.visits + 1>>
Counted on you rather than on me, so every tester reads the same number.
<<jump area_memory>>

== check_memory_visited
visited("check_memory_marker") is {visited("check_memory_marker")}. Expect false until you go there, then true.
<<jump area_memory>>

== check_memory_marker
Nothing happens here. It exists to be visited, so visited() has something to answer about.
<<jump area_memory>>

== check_memory_reset
<<set $met = false>>
Forgotten. The line above should go back to saying we have not met.
<<jump area_memory>>

# ---- the player -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Three checks the dialogue decides for itself where it can. Two of these were gaps until now for the same
# stated reason -- "it depends on the player" -- which was true of the naive way of asking and not of the
# question itself. perm() cannot be tested by giving one player two permission states, but it can be caught
# answering the same way to a node they hold and a node nobody does. knows() cannot be tested against whatever
# this player happens to have learned, but the recipe can be forgotten first.

== area_player
The player themselves. Each of these checks itself where it can, so you mostly just read what happened.
-> The names in text <<if not checked("player.npc.names")>>
    <<jump check_player_npc_names>>
-> Learning a recipe <<if not checked("learn.recipe")>>
    <<jump check_learn_recipe>>
-> Back
    <<jump start_more2>>

== check_player_npc_names
{player}, I am {npc}. Your name should be your own and mine should be this tester's, and neither should read as braces or as the other one.
-> Back
    <<jump area_player>>

== check_learn_recipe
A recipe you do not know yet: the Alchemy Cauldron. I will make sure you have forgotten it first, so this does not depend on what you already knew.
<<forget_recipe Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0>>
<<if knows("Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0")>>
    <<jump check_learn_recipe_bad>>
<<endif>>
<<learn Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0>>
<<if knows("Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0")>>
    <<jump check_learn_recipe_ok>>
<<else>>
    <<jump check_learn_recipe_bad>>
<<endif>>

== check_learn_recipe_ok
knows() was false before and is true now. Now the client half: open your crafting menu and check the Alchemy Cauldron is listed. /harness feedback if not.
-> Back
    <<jump area_player>>

== check_learn_recipe_bad
Wrong, or my assertion is. What each one actually answers now:
  knows() = {knows("Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0")}, read directly = {probe_knows("Alchemy_Cauldron_Recipe_Generated_0")}
Recorded as a failure already, and written to the log.
-> Back
    <<jump area_player>>

# ---- the NPC's own state --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This role inherits Idle and Stopped from Template_Temple. The check decides itself: the state is read back
# after each change, so nobody has to judge whether an NPC's behaviour looks different.

